Key Points Discussed
• The current recurring status meeting time is no longer working well for some participants. A move to Monday or another time early in the week was discussed to improve attendance.
• The team revisited last week’s discussion on the risk register and reviewed additions, removals, and areas that still need refinement.
• AI-related risks were discussed in two dimensions: internal organizational use of AI and external threats enabled by AI. The internal risk draft already covers unauthorized disclosure of sensitive information and use of third-party AI systems.
• The speaker noted that AI use is tightly governed within Abbott and that Core Diagnostics has a senior leader and council structure overseeing AI-related requests and exceptions.
• Upcoming GenAI and agentic AI policy documents were referenced as future inputs that should be incorporated into the risk and control documentation once available.
• Additional risk areas identified for better coverage included physical security, insider threat, cloud-related risks, and third-party supply chain or partner security risks.
• A key objective is to ensure the risk register ties cleanly into the Statement of Applicability (SOA), so every listed control has a corresponding risk rationale and related justification.
• The group discussed the idea that every control should be traceable back to a risk, and that unmatched SOA controls may indicate incompletely identified risks.
• The team is also preparing to move from risk work into objectives and metrics once the current risk-register and SOA alignment activities are complete.
• A broader yearly tracking document exists for certification maintenance tasks, nonconformities, audits, control monitoring, evidence refreshes, and related obligations. This will be shared for review.
• The SOC 2 review is currently focused on reviewing narratives and evidence. Comments were entered in the review column, with a second layer of review underway.
• A major concern is that several SOC 2 controls still name Syed/Sai or the prior support model as control owner, even though support responsibilities may now sit elsewhere. This raises the possibility of outdated narratives or potential control gaps.
• The highest-risk area appears to be the IT operations section, especially customer support, operational incident management, incident review meetings, metrics, and cloud inventory management.
• The team discussed the difference between ISO and SOC 2 treatment of control gaps. ISO is more tolerant when an issue is known and being fixed, while SOC 2 expects the described control environment to be in place across the audit period.
• Evidence quality expectations were clarified. Screenshots should ideally include a visible date/time or other context showing reproducibility, timing, and the system/source they came from.
• Existing evidence may not need to be recollected immediately if the team can reproduce the same evidence on demand during walkthroughs or audit requests.
• A mock SOC 2 audit by Coalfire is planned for late June. It will simulate external audit interviews, evidence requests, and control testing to help the team prepare for the actual audit.
Decisions Taken
• The recurring status meeting should likely be rescheduled to a different time, with Monday being a preferred option to improve attendance.
• The risk register will be expanded to include clearer coverage for AI, insider threat, cloud, physical security, and third-party/supply-chain related risks.
• Risk-register entries must be explicitly mapped back to the SOA so that risks, controls, and justifications form a consistent three-way linkage.
• SOC 2 narrative and evidence review will continue using the current review-comments approach, with remaining controls to be reviewed in sequence.
• The team will specifically investigate whether current operational controls described in SOC 2 narratives still exist as written and whether the listed owners are accurate.
• Evidence management will move toward storing attachments in the designated folder structure, with links or centralized storage allowed where appropriate.
• The mock audit with Coalfire will be used as a readiness check ahead of the formal SOC 2 audit.
